Response to the challenge

Short answer: the concentration study fails your test. I would drop it as the lead use case. The better question is your second one, “where is the organisation repeatedly attempting to solve the same underlying problem?”, and it is where the incident, issue and action data combine in a way a dashboard cannot.

Challenging my replacement
Question	Honest answer
What does concentration find that BI cannot?	Very little. Ranking incidents and loss by process, product or application is a pivot table. The only AI contribution is grouping by mechanism rather than by label, and that is a small improvement on a question the business already answers.
What decision changes?	Weak. “Look harder at the top loss areas” is already the default.
What would surprise a risk executive?	Probably not the hotspots. Concentration tells them where it hurts, and says nothing about whether the organisation is learning.

I also pushed the work toward a cell view (process by product by application) because it was easier to build and validate. That was the same mistake as the closure triage, in a different direction.

Why the second question is better

BI works on what is tagged and linked. Here, the tags are per object type, the taxonomies drifted over ten years, and the human cross-references are incomplete. So no dashboard can follow one problem from the incidents that exposed it, through the issues raised, to the actions taken and the incidents that came after. Reading the text across all four object types and joining by meaning is the one thing here that is hard without a language model. The unit of analysis is a problem thread: a failure mechanism in a stated context, with every incident, issue and action that touches it, ordered in time.

The closure triage looked at one link (issue to action). The thread looks at the whole chain, and it asks about the organisation’s learning rather than the quality of one closure.

What a thread can show that a dashboard cannot

These are hypotheses. The data may show the opposite.

Candidate insight	Why BI cannot see it
Fragmented ownership of one problem: the same mechanism raised as many separate issues across business units and years, each closed locally, never treated as one	Issues are counted by unit and label. Different labels and units hide the sameness.
Orphaned mechanisms: a mechanism with material consequence and no issue ever raised against it	Needs a match by meaning between incident text and issue text, not by recorded links
Repeated intervention class: the same type of action (for example training or procedure update) applied against the same mechanism several times, with incidents still recorded afterwards	Needs action text classified and tied to the mechanism. This is an association, not proof the actions failed.
Learning latency: time from the first recorded incident of a mechanism to the first issue, and from first issue to any enterprise-level response	Needs the thread to know when the mechanism first appeared
Migrating problems: a mechanism addressed in one process that later appears in another	Needs mechanism matching across processes
Relabelled reopenings: the same problem raised again under a new issue with a different root cause label	Needs text-based matching

An illustrative slide (placeholders only, not findings): “One failure mechanism: [N] issues and [M] actions across [J] business units over [Y] years, under [K] root cause labels, never managed as one problem. [X] incidents were recorded over the same period.”

A CRO would say “we did not know that” because the finding is about how the organisation learns, not where it hurts.

Decisions that change
Decision	Decision-maker	Trigger
Assign one enterprise owner and one remediation approach to a fragmented problem	CRO, executive risk committee	A thread with many independent issues across units, expert-confirmed
Change the intervention for a problem where the same action type has been repeated	Remediation policy owner, thread owner	Repeated intervention class with continued recorded events
Raise a new issue on an orphaned high-consequence mechanism	Head of Non-Financial Risk	Orphan thread confirmed
Fund or stop effort at problem level instead of issue level	Executive committee at planning	Thread-level view of effort against consequence

These decisions need no evidence about closure. They need the pattern across issues, which the data does hold.

The kill test, before building anything

The risk is that the existing structure already connects most of this. So first measure how much the AI adds.

Build threads two ways: from recorded Cross Ref links plus shared root cause labels (the baseline), and from text meaning.
Count threads that the text method connects and the baseline does not. These are the candidate surprises.
Have two experts independently judge a sample: is this one problem, and did they already know it was?
Proposed pass (to agree before the run): at least five threads of three or more issues across two or more business units, connected by no recorded link or shared label, confirmed by both experts, and judged by the sponsor as not previously known.

If the baseline already finds most of them, or experts cannot agree on what counts as one problem, the proposition fails cheaply, and that is useful to know.

Risks I would hold to
Similar text is not the same problem. Threads are model-derived and need expert confirmation. A thread is a hypothesis until confirmed.
Repeated attempts can be legitimate. Different systems, products or exposures may justify separate issues. The output flags candidates, not duplication.
No claims about effectiveness. “Recorded events continued after the action” is an association. It does not say the actions failed.
Left-censored history. If the data starts partway through a problem’s life, latency figures understate it.
Reporting bias affects which events appear, as before.
Action counts are a poor proxy for effort. Use them as counts only, not as cost.
No people or team ranking. Threads cross units by design, so ownership findings are about structure, not performance.
Would I fund it?

Yes, as a time-boxed study with the kill test, ahead of any platform. It is more complex than the concentration study, because it needs mechanism tagging and thread assembly. It is simpler than the closure triage, because there is no temporal anchoring, no action-set mapping and no review queue.

Controls stay out, as you asked. They could attach to a confirmed thread later.

I would keep two earlier pieces: concentration as a supporting materiality view (to rank threads, not as a finding), and the closure-review design on the shelf for threads where fragmented remediation is confirmed.

One decision for you

Name a sponsor who would take a confirmed thread to the planning cycle and say what they would change. If no one will, even this use case struggles. If one will, I would start with a two-week design for the kill test: how to build the baseline, how to select the expert sample, and how experts will label “same problem”.
